{"file_type":"RF_VCB_PROJECT","file_version":2,"title":"","creation_date":1749068803,"last_edit_date":1794784381,"time_worked_on":190471,"layers":["","",""],"assembly":"# Advent of Code 2025, day 2 part 2 (Gift Shop)\n#\n# THE PUZZLE\n# The input is a list of ID ranges. In part 2 an ID is invalid when it is some digits repeated at least\n# twice: 12341234 (1234 two times), 123123123 (123 three times), 1111111 (1 seven times). The answer is\n# the sum of all invalid IDs inside the ranges.\n#\n# THE IDEA\n# An invalid ID of n digits made of a block of k digits is  h * M  with h a k-digit number, where M is a 1\n# every k digits:\n#     12341234  = 1234 * 10001       (n = 8, k = 4)\n#     123123123 = 123 * 1001001      (n = 9, k = 3)\n#     M = (10^n - 1) / (10^k - 1)\n# A FAMILY is one pair (n, k). Inside a family the invalid IDs are evenly spaced, M apart:\n#     n = 6, k = 2:   101010, 111111, 121212, ..., 999999      step 10101\n# The program never looks at an ID that is not invalid. For each range and each family it jumps to the\n# first candidate that is not below the range's first ID, then adds them up one step at a time until it\n# passes the range's last ID.\n#\n# COUNTED TWICE\n# Some IDs are in two families: 222222 is 222 two times and also 22 three times. Up to 10 digits that\n# only happens for 6 and for 10 digits, and the IDs in question are exactly those of one repeated digit\n# (111111, 222222, ...). So the table has a family for k = 1 at 6 and at 10 digits whose SIGN is 1: its\n# IDs are added to NEG instead of SUM, and at the end SUM = SUM - NEG.\n#     6 digits:   + (k = 3)  + (k = 2)  - (k = 1)\n#     10 digits:  + (k = 5)  + (k = 2)  - (k = 1)\n# For 2, 3, 5 and 7 digits the only block length is 1; for 4, 8 and 9 digits the families with a shorter\n# block lie inside the one with the longest block, so only that one is in the table.\n#\n# THE WHOLE PROGRAM IN PSEUDO CODE\n#     SUM = 0; NEG = 0\n#     for each range LO..HI in the input:\n#         for each family in K_TABLE:               # 13 families, 2 to 10 digits\n#             C = smallest candidate of the family  # 10^(k-1) * M\n#             LAST = largest candidate              # 10^n - 1\n#             if LAST < LO: continue                # the range lies above the whole family\n#             for S in M*10^(k-1), ..., M*10, M:    # big steps first\n#                 while C + S < LO: C = C + S       # STEP_UP: at most 9 times per step size\n#             # now C is the largest candidate below LO (or the smallest candidate, if that is not below LO)\n#             if C < LO: C = C + S                  # S is M here: the first candidate in the range\n#             while C <= HI and C <= LAST:\n#                 if SIGN == 0: SUM = SUM + C\n#                 else:         NEG = NEG + C\n#                 C = C + S\n#     SUM = SUM - NEG\n#     write SUM as decimal digits                   # with the same STEP_UP, see ALL_RANGES_DONE\n#     stop\n#\n# The CPU cannot multiply or divide, so the candidates and step sizes of every family are in a table at\n# 0x3000 (K_TABLE), and \"jump to the first candidate\" is done by walking up in decimal steps.\n#\n# THE RESULT, when the CPU stops on the Breakpoint at END\n#   0x0100-0x0102  the sum as three 16-bit words, low word first\n#   0x0110-0x011E  the sum as 15 decimal digits, one per word, most significant first\n#   (0x0104-0x0106 holds NEG, the part that was subtracted)\n#\n# THE INPUT (rule 1 of RULES.md), at 0x2000\n# Each range is its first and last ID. Each ID is three 16-bit words, low word first. Three zero words\n# end the list. Built-in limits: IDs of at most 10 digits, a sum below 2^48.\n#\n# NUMBERS WIDER THAN A REGISTER\n# IDs go up to 9,999,999,999, which needs 34 bits; the sum needs more. A number is therefore three\n# memory words of 16 bits: NAME_0 (lowest), NAME_1, NAME_2. The CPU's registers are 32 bits wide, so two\n# words can be added in a register without losing the carry: the carry ends up in the upper half, rgAH.\n#\n# THE FOUR IDIOMS used everywhere below (v is a variable's address, below 0x1000)\n#     getA v                   A = the word at v, upper half 0   (lia v; load rgA rgA: lia puts the address in A, load replaces it)\n#     getB v                   B = the word at v, upper half 0\n#     putA v                   the word at v = the lower half of A   (this uses up B)\n#     carryB                   B = the upper half of A: the carry of the addition just made\n# What each instruction does exactly is in findings/cpu-instructions.md.\n#\n# MEMORY MAP\n#   0x0100-0x0165  variables            0x2000-  the input\n#   0x1000-        the main program     0x3000-  K_TABLE: candidates and steps per family\n#   0x1800-        subroutines          0x3100-  POWERS: powers of ten for the decimal digits\n#   0xFF00         where the CPU starts\n\n# ---- The CPU's instructions as symbols (day 1's table without its _32 names, which the CPU ignores) ----\nsymbol nop 0b0000_0000_0000_0000            # the word 0: stops the CPU (the same word as non)\nsymbol ld  0b0010_0000_0000_0000            # load a register from memory (used through the load macro)\nsymbol lia 0b0011_0000_0000_0000            # lia n: A = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol lib 0b0100_0000_0000_0000            # lib n: B = n (0 to 4095), upper half 0\nsymbol sto 0b0101_0000_0000_0000            # store a register to memory (used through the store macro)\n\nmacro  load  { ld  | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # load X Y: X = the word at the address in Y\nmacro  store { sto | a? | (b? >> 6) }       # store X Y: the word at the address in Y = X\n\nsymbol sum    0b0110_0100_0000_0000         # A = A + B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol sub    0b0110_0010_0000_0000         # A = A - B (32 bits), sets zero, carry, nega\nsymbol shf    0b0110_0001_0000_0000         # shf | k: A = A shifted left by k (0 to 15)\nsymbol and    0b0110_0000_1000_0000         # A = A and B\nsymbol or     0b0110_0000_0100_0000         # A = A or B\nsymbol xor    0b0110_0000_0010_0000         # A = A xor B\nsymbol neg    0b0110_0000_0001_0000         # A = every bit of A flipped\n\nsymbol psh 0b1010_0000_0000_0000            # psh X: push the 16 bits of X\nsymbol pop 0b1011_0000_0000_0000            # pop X: pop 16 bits into X\n\nsymbol jmp  0b1100_0000_0000_0000           # jmp; L: go to L.  jmp c; L: go to L when c holds\nsymbol call 0b1110_0000_0000_0000           # call; L: run the subroutine at L\nsymbol ret  0b1111_0000_0000_0000           # return from a subroutine\n\n# The registers, as load, store, psh and pop name them\nsymbol rgAH 0b1000_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgA  0b0100_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of A\nsymbol rgBH 0b0010_0000_0000                # the upper 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgB  0b0001_0000_0000                # the lower 16 bits of B\nsymbol rgSP 0b0000_1000_0000                # the program counter (leave alone)\nsymbol rgSB 0b0000_0100_0000                # the stack pointer (leave alone)\nsymbol non  0                               # stops the CPU (Breakpoint): the end of the program\n\n# The conditions of jmp\nsymbol zero  0b1000_0000_0000               # the last sum or sub gave 0\nsymbol carry 0b0100_0000_0000               # the last sum carried, or the last sub did not borrow (A >= B)\nsymbol nega  0b0010_0000_0000               # the last sub went below 0 (A < B)\nsymbol eq    0b0001_0000_0000               # A == B right now\nsymbol neq   0b0000_1000_0000               # A != B right now\n\n# ---- Macros for the common operations (explained in the README). A macro cannot use another macro, so\n#      load and store are spelled out here. v: a variable, p: a variable holding an address, L: a label ----\nmacro getA    { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6) }                                              # A = v\nmacro getB    { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6) }                                              # B = v\nmacro putA    { lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                                             # v = A (uses B)\nmacro putAat  { lib a?; ld | rgB | (rgB >> 6); sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }                      # the word p points at = A (uses B)\nmacro inc     { lia a?; ld | rgA | (rgA >> 6); lib 1; sum; lib a?; sto | rgA | (rgB >> 6) }  # v = v + 1 (uses A and B)\nmacro goto    { jmp; a? }                                                                    # go to L\nmacro if_eq   { jmp eq; a? }                                                                 # go to L when A == B\nmacro if_neq  { jmp neq; a? }                                                                # go to L when A != B\nmacro do      { call; a? }                                                                   # call the subroutine L\nmacro carryB  { psh rgAH; pop rgB }                                                          # B = the carry of the last sum\nmacro if_nega { jmp nega; a? }                                                               # go to L after a sub when A < B\n\n# ---- Variables (all below 0x1000, so lia/lib can address them) ----\n# The result\npointer SUM_0 0x0100 0\npointer SUM_1 0x0101 0\npointer SUM_2 0x0102 0\n# What must be subtracted from SUM at the end\npointer NEG_0 0x0104 0\npointer NEG_1 0x0105 0\npointer NEG_2 0x0106 0\n# The current range: its first and last ID. HI must follow LO.\npointer LO_0 0x0120 0\npointer LO_1 0x0121 0\npointer LO_2 0x0122 0\npointer HI_0 0x0123 0\npointer HI_1 0x0124 0\npointer HI_2 0x0125 0\n# The current family: NEXT is where the next family starts in the table, SIGN is 1 when its IDs are\n# subtracted, C the candidate ID, LAST the family's largest candidate. SIGN, C and LAST must follow NEXT.\npointer NEXT 0x0130 0\npointer SIGN 0x0131 0\npointer C_0 0x0132 0\npointer C_1 0x0133 0\npointer C_2 0x0134 0\npointer LAST_0 0x0135 0\npointer LAST_1 0x0136 0\npointer LAST_2 0x0137 0\n# S is the current step, T a trial value, COUNT how often STEP_UP stepped\npointer S_0 0x0140 0\npointer S_1 0x0141 0\npointer S_2 0x0142 0\npointer T_0 0x0143 0\npointer T_1 0x0144 0\npointer T_2 0x0145 0\npointer COUNT 0x0146 0\n# The constant 1 as a number\npointer ONE_0 0x0150 1\npointer ONE_1 0x0151 0\npointer ONE_2 0x0152 0\n# Pointers: SRC and DST for COPY_1, IN the next input word, DIGIT the next digit to write,\n# and the addresses of the two tables\npointer SRC 0x0160 0\npointer DST 0x0161 0\npointer IN 0x0162 0x2000\npointer DIGIT 0x0163 0x0110\npointer K_TABLE 0x0164 0x3000\npointer POWERS 0x0165 0x3100\n\n# ---- The CPU starts at 0xFF00 ----\norigin 0xFF00\ngoto MAIN\n\norigin 0x1000\n@ MAIN\n# ==== for each range LO..HI in the input ====\n#     LO = input[IN], HI = input[IN + 3]; IN = IN + 6\n#     if LO == 0: goto ALL_RANGES_DONE\n#     SRC = K_TABLE                      # start again at the first family\n# COPY_3 copies three words from the address in SRC to the address in DST and moves both on, so two\n# calls fill LO and then HI, which lies right behind LO.\n@ NEXT_RANGE\n\tgetA IN; putA SRC                                                                    # SRC = IN\n\tlia LO_0; putA DST                                                                   # DST = the address of LO\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # LO = the range's first ID\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # HI = the range's last ID\n\tgetA SRC; putA IN                                                                    # IN = SRC, now at the next range\n\t# A first ID of 0 ends the list: OR the three words of LO together and compare with 0.\n\t# (eq compares A with B as they are now, so no subtraction is needed.)\n\tgetA LO_0; getB LO_1; or; getB LO_2; or; lib 0\n\tif_eq ALL_RANGES_DONE\n\tgetA K_TABLE; putA SRC                                                               # SRC = the start of the table: the first family\n\n# ==== for each family in the table ====\n# SRC points at the family's table entry: [address of the next entry] [SIGN] [smallest candidate]\n# [largest candidate] [step sizes, largest first]. NEXT, SIGN, C and LAST lie behind each other, so\n# four copies fill them.\n#     NEXT = table[SRC]; SIGN; C = smallest candidate; LAST = largest candidate\n#     if NEXT == 0: goto NEXT_RANGE      # the table is finished: all families done for this range\n#     if LAST < LO: goto SKIP_K          # every candidate of this family is below the range\n@ NEXT_K\n\tlia NEXT; putA DST                                                                   # DST = the address of NEXT\n\tdo COPY_1                                                                            # NEXT = where the next family's entry starts\n\tdo COPY_1                                                                            # SIGN = 0 to add this family, 1 to subtract it\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # C = the smallest candidate of this family\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # LAST = the largest candidate of this family\n\tgetA NEXT; lib 0                                                                     # NEXT == 0 marks the end of the table\n\tif_eq NEXT_RANGE\n\t# No candidate of this family is in the range when even the largest is below LO\n\t# compare LAST with LO, highest word first; afterwards: nega if LAST < LO, zero if equal\n\tgetA LAST_2; getB LO_2; if_neq CMP_1                                                 # word 2 differs: it decides\n\tgetA LAST_1; getB LO_1; if_neq CMP_1                                                 # word 1 differs: it decides\n\tgetA LAST_0; getB LO_0                                                               # otherwise word 0 decides\n@ CMP_1\n\tsub                                                                                  # A - B sets the flags zero and nega\n\tif_nega SKIP_K\n\n# ==== walk C up to the largest candidate below LO ====\n#     while SRC != NEXT:                 # the step sizes are the rest of this family's entry\n#         S = table[SRC]; SRC = SRC + 3\n#         STEP_UP                        # while C + S < LO: C = C + S\n# Example, 4 digits (M = 101) and LO = 3308: C starts at 1010. Step 1010: 2020, 3030 (4040 is too far).\n# Step 101: 3131, 3232 (3333 is too far). C = 3232 is the largest candidate below 3308.\n# This is long division done by walking: it finds h in  h * M  one decimal digit at a time.\n@ NEXT_STEP\n\tgetA SRC; getB NEXT                                                                  # A = SRC, B = NEXT\n\tif_eq STEPS_DONE                                                                     # SRC reached the next entry: no step sizes left\n\tlia S_0; putA DST                                                                    # DST = the address of S\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # S = the next step size\n\tdo STEP_UP                                                                           # while C + S < LO: C = C + S\n\tgoto NEXT_STEP\n\n# ==== step onto the first candidate that is not below LO ====\n# S is now the smallest step, M: the distance between two candidates.\n#     if C < LO: C = C + S\n# (C is not below LO only when even the family's smallest candidate is at or above LO.)\n@ STEPS_DONE\n\t# compare C with LO, highest word first; afterwards: nega if C < LO, zero if equal\n\tgetA C_2; getB LO_2; if_neq CMP_2                                                    # word 2 differs: it decides\n\tgetA C_1; getB LO_1; if_neq CMP_2                                                    # word 1 differs: it decides\n\tgetA C_0; getB LO_0                                                                  # otherwise word 0 decides\n@ CMP_2\n\tsub                                                                                  # A - B sets the flags zero and nega\n\tif_nega ONE_MORE                                                                     # C < LO: one more step\n\tgoto ADD_CANDIDATES                                                                  # C is already at or above LO\n@ ONE_MORE\n\t# C = C + S      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA C_0; getB S_0; sum; putA C_0                                                    # word 0: A = C_0 + S_0\n\tcarryB; getA C_1; sum; getB S_1; sum; putA C_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + C_1 + S_1\n\tcarryB; getA C_2; sum; getB S_2; sum; putA C_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + C_2 + S_2\n\n# ==== add up the candidates inside the range ====\n#     while C <= HI and C <= LAST:\n#         if SIGN == 0: SUM = SUM + C    # ADD_TO_SUM\n#         else:         NEG = NEG + C    # a family that only exists to undo double counting\n#         C = C + S                      # STEP_ON\n# The tests are written the other way round: leave when HI < C, leave when LAST < C.\n# Leaving goes to NEXT_K; SRC already points at the next family's entry.\n@ ADD_CANDIDATES\n\t# compare HI with C, highest word first; afterwards: nega if HI < C, zero if equal\n\tgetA HI_2; getB C_2; if_neq CMP_3                                                    # word 2 differs: it decides\n\tgetA HI_1; getB C_1; if_neq CMP_3                                                    # word 1 differs: it decides\n\tgetA HI_0; getB C_0                                                                  # otherwise word 0 decides\n@ CMP_3\n\tsub                                                                                  # A - B sets the flags zero and nega\n\tif_nega NEXT_K                                                                       # HI < C: past the end of the range\n\t# compare LAST with C, highest word first; afterwards: nega if LAST < C, zero if equal\n\tgetA LAST_2; getB C_2; if_neq CMP_4                                                  # word 2 differs: it decides\n\tgetA LAST_1; getB C_1; if_neq CMP_4                                                  # word 1 differs: it decides\n\tgetA LAST_0; getB C_0                                                                # otherwise word 0 decides\n@ CMP_4\n\tsub                                                                                  # A - B sets the flags zero and nega\n\tif_nega NEXT_K                                                                       # LAST < C: past the family's largest candidate\n\tgetA SIGN; lib 0                                                                     # A = SIGN, B = 0\n\tif_eq ADD_TO_SUM                                                                     # SIGN == 0: this family is added\n\t# NEG = NEG + C      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA NEG_0; getB C_0; sum; putA NEG_0                                                # word 0: A = NEG_0 + C_0\n\tcarryB; getA NEG_1; sum; getB C_1; sum; putA NEG_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + NEG_1 + C_1\n\tcarryB; getA NEG_2; sum; getB C_2; sum; putA NEG_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + NEG_2 + C_2\n\tgoto STEP_ON                                                                         # (SIGN == 1 was added to NEG)\n@ ADD_TO_SUM\n\t# SUM = SUM + C      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA SUM_0; getB C_0; sum; putA SUM_0                                                # word 0: A = SUM_0 + C_0\n\tcarryB; getA SUM_1; sum; getB C_1; sum; putA SUM_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + SUM_1 + C_1\n\tcarryB; getA SUM_2; sum; getB C_2; sum; putA SUM_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + SUM_2 + C_2\n@ STEP_ON\n\t# C = C + S      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA C_0; getB S_0; sum; putA C_0                                                    # word 0: A = C_0 + S_0\n\tcarryB; getA C_1; sum; getB S_1; sum; putA C_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + C_1 + S_1\n\tcarryB; getA C_2; sum; getB S_2; sum; putA C_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + C_2 + S_2\n\tgoto ADD_CANDIDATES\n\n# Skip this family without reading its step sizes:  SRC = NEXT\n@ SKIP_K\n\tgetA NEXT; putA SRC                                                                  # SRC = NEXT\n\tgoto NEXT_K\n\n# ==== finish: SUM = SUM - NEG, then write SUM as decimal digits ====\n# A digit is how often a power of ten fits into what is left. Without division that is counting:\n#     SUM = SUM - NEG\n#     LO = SUM + 1; C = 0\n#     for S in 10^14, 10^13, ..., 10, 1:\n#         STEP_UP                        # while C + S < LO: C = C + S; COUNT = COUNT + 1\n#         memory[DIGIT] = COUNT; DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n# C climbs from 0 to exactly SUM, and COUNT is one decimal digit each time. \"C + S < SUM + 1\" is\n# \"C + S <= SUM\", which is why LO is set to SUM + 1: STEP_UP only knows \"below LO\".\n@ ALL_RANGES_DONE\n\t# SUM = SUM - NEG, word by word, lowest first. When a word needs to borrow, the 32-bit result is\n\t# negative and rgAH reads 0xFFFF. Copying rgAH into both halves of B makes B = -1 (or 0 when there\n\t# was no borrow), and adding that to the next word takes the borrow off.\n\tgetA SUM_0; getB NEG_0; sub; putA SUM_0                                              # word 0: A = SUM_0 - NEG_0\n\tcarryB; psh rgAH; pop rgBH; getA SUM_1; sum; getB NEG_1; sub; putA SUM_1                                                     # word 1: A = SUM_1 + borrow (0 or -1) - NEG_1\n\tcarryB; psh rgAH; pop rgBH; getA SUM_2; sum; getB NEG_2; sub; putA SUM_2                                                     # word 2: A = SUM_2 + borrow (0 or -1) - NEG_2\n\t# LO = SUM + ONE      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA SUM_0; getB ONE_0; sum; putA LO_0                                               # word 0: A = SUM_0 + ONE_0\n\tcarryB; getA SUM_1; sum; getB ONE_1; sum; putA LO_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + SUM_1 + ONE_1\n\tcarryB; getA SUM_2; sum; getB ONE_2; sum; putA LO_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + SUM_2 + ONE_2\n\tlia 0; putA C_0; putA C_1; putA C_2                                                  # C = 0\n\tgetA POWERS; putA SRC                                                                # SRC = the table of powers of ten\n@ NEXT_DIGIT\n\tlia S_0; putA DST                                                                    # DST = the address of S\n\tdo COPY_3                                                                            # S = the next power of ten\n\t# A power of 0 ends the table\n\tgetA S_0; getB S_1; or; getB S_2; or; lib 0\n\tif_eq END\n\tdo STEP_UP                                                                           # COUNT = how often S fits\n\tgetA COUNT; putAat DIGIT                                                             # memory[DIGIT] = COUNT   (B holds the address in DIGIT)\n\tinc DIGIT                                                                            # DIGIT = DIGIT + 1\n\tgoto NEXT_DIGIT\n\n# The word 0 stops the CPU on its Breakpoint.\n@ END\n\tnon\n\n# ---- Subroutines ----\norigin 0x1800\n# STEP_UP: add S to C as often as the result stays below LO. COUNT is the number of steps.\n#     COUNT = 0\n#     loop:\n#         T = C + S                      # try the step in T first\n#         if not T < LO: return          # too far: C stays as it is\n#         C = T; COUNT = COUNT + 1\n# Used twice: to walk a candidate up to a range (COUNT is ignored), and to produce decimal digits.\n@ STEP_UP\n\tlia 0; putA COUNT                                                                    # COUNT = 0\n@ STEP_UP_AGAIN\n\t# T = C + S      (three words, lowest first; the carry travels in rgAH)\n\tgetA C_0; getB S_0; sum; putA T_0                                                    # word 0: A = C_0 + S_0\n\tcarryB; getA C_1; sum; getB S_1; sum; putA T_1                                                     # word 1: A = carry + C_1 + S_1\n\tcarryB; getA C_2; sum; getB S_2; sum; putA T_2                                                     # word 2: A = carry + C_2 + S_2\n\t# compare T with LO, highest word first; afterwards: nega if T < LO, zero if equal\n\tgetA T_2; getB LO_2; if_neq CMP_5                                                    # word 2 differs: it decides\n\tgetA T_1; getB LO_1; if_neq CMP_5                                                    # word 1 differs: it decides\n\tgetA T_0; getB LO_0                                                                  # otherwise word 0 decides\n@ CMP_5\n\tsub                                                                                  # A - B sets the flags zero and nega\n\tif_nega STEP_UP_TAKE                                                                 # T < LO: take the step\n\tret                                                                                  # otherwise done\n@ STEP_UP_TAKE\n\t# C = T, word by word\n\tgetA T_0; putA C_0; getA T_1; putA C_1; getA T_2; putA C_2\n\tinc COUNT                                                                            # COUNT = COUNT + 1\n\tgoto STEP_UP_AGAIN\n\n# COPY_3: copy three words (one number) from the address in SRC to the address in DST; both move on by three.\n#     repeat 3 times: memory[DST] = memory[SRC]; SRC = SRC + 1; DST = DST + 1\n@ COPY_3\n\tdo COPY_1\n\tdo COPY_1\n\tdo COPY_1\n\tret\n\n# COPY_1: copy one word from the address in SRC to the address in DST; both move on by one.\n#     memory[DST] = memory[SRC]; SRC = SRC + 1; DST = DST + 1\n# SRC and DST are pointers: variables that hold an address. Reading through one takes two loads:\n# first the address into B, then the word at that address.\n@ COPY_1\n\tgetB SRC; load rgA rgB; putAat DST                                                   # A = memory[SRC]; memory[DST] = A\n\tinc SRC                                                                              # SRC = SRC + 1\n\tinc DST                                                                              # DST = DST + 1\n\tret\n\n# ---- The table of families ----\n# One entry per family:\n#     1 word    the address of the next entry (0 in the last, empty entry)\n#     1 word    SIGN: 0 = add the family to SUM, 1 = add it to NEG (to be subtracted at the end)\n#     3 words   the smallest candidate, 10^(k-1) * M             e.g. 4 digits: 10 * 101 = 1010\n#     3 words   the largest candidate, 10^n - 1                   e.g. 4 digits: 9999\n#     3 words each: the steps M * 10^j for j = k-1 down to 0     e.g. 4 digits: 1010, 101\n# A number is written low word first: 1000010000 = 0x3B9A_F110 is  0xF110; 0x3B9A; 0x0000.\norigin 0x3000\n# 2 digits, a block of 1 repeated 2 times: M = 11, candidates 11 to 99\n0x300B; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x000B; 0x0000; 0x0000        # smallest 11\n0x0063; 0x0000; 0x0000        # largest 99\n0x000B; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 11\n# 3 digits, a block of 1 repeated 3 times: M = 111, candidates 111 to 999\n0x3016; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x006F; 0x0000; 0x0000        # smallest 111\n0x03E7; 0x0000; 0x0000        # largest 999\n0x006F; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 111\n# 4 digits, a block of 2 repeated 2 times: M = 101, candidates 1010 to 9999\n0x3024; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x03F2; 0x0000; 0x0000        # smallest 1010\n0x270F; 0x0000; 0x0000        # largest 9999\n0x03F2; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 1010\n0x0065; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 101\n# 5 digits, a block of 1 repeated 5 times: M = 11111, candidates 11111 to 99999\n0x302F; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x2B67; 0x0000; 0x0000        # smallest 11111\n0x869F; 0x0001; 0x0000        # largest 99999\n0x2B67; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 11111\n# 6 digits, a block of 3 repeated 2 times: M = 1001, candidates 100100 to 999999\n0x3040; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x8704; 0x0001; 0x0000        # smallest 100100\n0x423F; 0x000F; 0x0000        # largest 999999\n0x8704; 0x0001; 0x0000        # step 100100\n0x271A; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 10010\n0x03E9; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 1001\n# 6 digits, a block of 2 repeated 3 times: M = 10101, candidates 101010 to 999999\n0x304E; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x8A92; 0x0001; 0x0000        # smallest 101010\n0x423F; 0x000F; 0x0000        # largest 999999\n0x8A92; 0x0001; 0x0000        # step 101010\n0x2775; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 10101\n# 6 digits, a block of 1 repeated 6 times: M = 111111, candidates 111111 to 999999, subtracted\n0x3059; 1                     # next entry; SIGN = 1: subtracted\n0xB207; 0x0001; 0x0000        # smallest 111111\n0x423F; 0x000F; 0x0000        # largest 999999\n0xB207; 0x0001; 0x0000        # step 111111\n# 7 digits, a block of 1 repeated 7 times: M = 1111111, candidates 1111111 to 9999999\n0x3064; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0xF447; 0x0010; 0x0000        # smallest 1111111\n0x967F; 0x0098; 0x0000        # largest 9999999\n0xF447; 0x0010; 0x0000        # step 1111111\n# 8 digits, a block of 4 repeated 2 times: M = 10001, candidates 10001000 to 99999999\n0x3078; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x9A68; 0x0098; 0x0000        # smallest 10001000\n0xE0FF; 0x05F5; 0x0000        # largest 99999999\n0x9A68; 0x0098; 0x0000        # step 10001000\n0x42A4; 0x000F; 0x0000        # step 1000100\n0x86AA; 0x0001; 0x0000        # step 100010\n0x2711; 0x0000; 0x0000        # step 10001\n# 9 digits, a block of 3 repeated 3 times: M = 1001001, candidates 100100100 to 999999999\n0x3089; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0x6804; 0x05F7; 0x0000        # smallest 100100100\n0xC9FF; 0x3B9A; 0x0000        # largest 999999999\n0x6804; 0x05F7; 0x0000        # step 100100100\n0xBD9A; 0x0098; 0x0000        # step 10010010\n0x4629; 0x000F; 0x0000        # step 1001001\n# 10 digits, a block of 5 repeated 2 times: M = 100001, candidates 1000010000 to 9999999999\n0x30A0; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0xF110; 0x3B9A; 0x0000        # smallest 1000010000\n0xE3FF; 0x540B; 0x0002        # largest 9999999999\n0xF110; 0x3B9A; 0x0000        # step 1000010000\n0xE4E8; 0x05F5; 0x0000        # step 100001000\n0x96E4; 0x0098; 0x0000        # step 10000100\n0x424A; 0x000F; 0x0000        # step 1000010\n0x86A1; 0x0001; 0x0000        # step 100001\n# 10 digits, a block of 2 repeated 5 times: M = 101010101, candidates 1010101010 to 9999999999\n0x30AE; 0                     # next entry; SIGN = 0: added\n0xEB12; 0x3C34; 0x0000        # smallest 1010101010\n0xE3FF; 0x540B; 0x0002        # largest 9999999999\n0xEB12; 0x3C34; 0x0000        # step 1010101010\n0x4AB5; 0x0605; 0x0000        # step 101010101\n# 10 digits, a block of 1 repeated 10 times: M = 1111111111, candidates 1111111111 to 9999999999, subtracted\n0x30B9; 1                     # next entry; SIGN = 1: subtracted\n0x35C7; 0x423A; 0x0000        # smallest 1111111111\n0xE3FF; 0x540B; 0x0002        # largest 9999999999\n0x35C7; 0x423A; 0x0000        # step 1111111111\n0; 0; 0; 0; 0; 0; 0; 0                                                                # the end of the table: an entry whose next address is 0\n\n# ---- Powers of ten, 10^14 down to 1, then 0 ----\norigin 0x3100\n0x4000; 0x107A; 0x5AF3        # 10^14\n0xA000; 0x4E72; 0x0918        # 10^13\n0x1000; 0xD4A5; 0x00E8        # 10^12\n0xE800; 0x4876; 0x0017        # 10^11\n0xE400; 0x540B; 0x0002        # 10^10\n0xCA00; 0x3B9A; 0x0000        # 10^9\n0xE100; 0x05F5; 0x0000        # 10^8\n0x9680; 0x0098; 0x0000        # 10^7\n0x4240; 0x000F; 0x0000        #